From 4342503fd8b58317fb2796c91cc03a9dd0ca701a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ulises Gascon <ulisesgascongonzalez@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:44:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] tls: reject chains containing blocked public keys

Node.js embeds the Mozilla root store but has no equivalent of the
browsers' CRLSet/OneCRL and no default online revocation checking, so a
certificate chain through a compromised intermediate still verifies as
long as it reaches a trusted root. Removing the affected roots would
over-block, since other legitimate intermediates chain to them.

Add a small compiled-in blocklist of SubjectPublicKeyInfo SHA-256 hashes
and check every verified chain against it from a single
SSL_CTX_set_cert_verify_callback hook. A hit is reported as
X509_V_ERR_CERT_REVOKED through the normal verification path, so it
honours rejectUnauthorized and surfaces as authorizationError on both
client and server sockets. The hook is installed for every SSL_CTX
Node.js creates: SecureContext and QUIC.

The initial entries are the eight eMudhra/emSign intermediates whose
private keys were reported compromised in October 2026. The same list is
shipped by Go (CVE-2026-111943) and Electron.

Sessions resumed from before this change are not re-verified, as is
standard for TLS resumption; the exposure ends with the session timeout.

Test-only hooks on the crypto binding, reachable with
--expose-internals, allow the test to block a fixture key and exercise
the full handshake.

Refs: https://github.com/electron/electron/pull/55126
Refs: https://github.com/golang/go/issues/82345
Assisted-by: Claude, Codex
Signed-off-by: Ulises Gascon <ulisesgascongonzalez@gmail.com>
PR-URL: https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/66871
Reviewed-By: Filip Skokan <panva.ip@gmail.com>

Upstream-Commit: 4342503fd8b58317fb2796c91cc03a9dd0ca701a
Backport-Note: Adapt includes and binding registration to Node.js
22.18.0. Keep the upstream SPKI checks and eight hashes; bind test
